course: dictionary
lesson: 7689
title: What a protocol is
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A protocol is a set of rules or steps that must be followed. Read the words around it to find the meaning. In «In 1987 countries agreed in the Montreal Protocol to ban chemicals that damaged the ozone layer», the word countries agreed is a clue: a protocol is an agreement or set of rules.
explain: The meaning changes with the situation. «The school's fire protocol» means the steps to follow in a fire. «Royal protocol» means the formal manners for meeting a king. Ask what the rules are for, then swap in "set of rules" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In computing, protocols are shared rules that let computers talk. «Many networks joined it using shared rules called protocols, especially TCP/IP.» Without common rules, computers could not understand each other, so the internet could not grow.
fact: A protocol is a set of rules or steps that must be followed.
fact: «Protocol» can mean the official rules of correct behaviour on formal occasions, such as «royal protocol».
fact: «Protocol» can mean a set of steps for a certain situation, such as «the school's fire protocol».
fact: In computing, «protocols» are rules that let computers send and receive information.
fact: «Follow protocol» means do things the correct, agreed way; «break protocol» means do not.
exercise: «The school's fire protocol tells everyone which door to use.» What does protocol mean here? | A friendly chat with the teacher | A kind of school uniform | A type of fire engine | *A set of steps to follow in an emergency
exercise: «Royal protocol» tells you how to behave when meeting a king. What does protocol mean? | A food served at parties | A kind of royal crown | *Official rules of correct behaviour on formal occasions | A loud speech
exercise: «Internet protocol» lets computers talk to each other. What is a protocol in computing? | A fast game played online | Pictures stored on a phone | *Shared rules that computers use to send and receive information | A type of keyboard
test: «In 1969 ARPANET linked a few computers, and many networks joined it using shared rules called protocols.» Why did the networks need protocols? | So they could work without any computers | *So they could agree how to send information to each other | To change their colours | To make the computers smaller
test: «Countries agreed the Montreal Protocol to ban chemicals that damaged the ozone layer.» What is the Montreal Protocol? | A map of the ozone layer | A science club | A type of computer | *An agreement between countries
exam: Which meaning fits «follow protocol» in a science lab? | *Doing the exact agreed method in the right order | Breaking the rules on purpose | Playing a board game | Wearing a lab coat for fun
